bridgeT -- SEQ 489 (Clay, 2026-10-30): the bridge for a C++-only statement on the new road, PRE-REGISTERED ON TRUNK.
Two timings are tried on two branches, neither merged: bridge-run (the statement's C++ method runs at run time, in its
place) and bridge-parse (it fires at parse, as the old road's non-deferred rules do). Same rows on both.

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each under an alarm: everything above this
file's `stop();`, then the row on the OLD road (the statement itself) or on the NEW road (STARt called by name on the
same text, then testOP on what it handed back), then `BR NEXT` and a sentinel on stderr. Each row is read as
`stdout | R<lines starting REFUSED> x<exit>`, with any `Search list:` line read as S. The preamble's search lines carry
no `list`, so every S comes from a row.

THE OLD ROAD IS ALREADY MIXED (measured 2026-10-30): Search, DEBUG and DEF carry no defer, so they fire at parse; CerR,
CouT and ScopeXP are deferred and fire in place. So the old road is the control by value, not the intended value: it
prints S in an untaken arm (BR-7), prints S once for a three-pass loop (BR-8), and dies with exit 139 for search in a
taken arm or an else arm (BR-9, BR-10) and for a top-level `:qa;` (BR-4).

TODAY ON TRUNK (measured): the kant statement rules carry new names (SEARCh, DEBUg, CERr, ...), so the `aCTion`+name
lookup binds no C++ method; STARt's seq skips them with no word (BR-1, BR-5, BR-6), and If refuses an arm that handed up
no instruct (BR-7, BR-9, BR-10). DEf already fires at parse on the new road -- its define child is a C++ rule that runs
as it parses -- so BR-3 reads `9 |9 |` where the old road reads `2 |9 |`.

CERr and COUt are the no-silence rows: bound straight to aCTionCerR / aCTionCouT they print `5 0` -- the old print walk
cannot read a kant print item -- so the bridge refuses them by name until they move into PRINt.
DEBUg and a top-level SCOPEXp have no witness at this seat (debug toggles a trace flag; a top-level scope has no method to
bind into), so BR-2 and BR-4 assert only that the run reaches its end and nothing refuses.

